  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Generally quiet pattern statewide looks to \npersist through Saturday with most precipitation chances \nconfined to the southern tier of the state.\n\nFinally getting an evening with nothing on the radar as of \nmid evening. Expect the overnight to remain dry. Any showers\nare expected to be after sunrise on Friday.\n\nMore information below.\n\nThe upper level jet streak is anticipated to traverse through \nOntario and into New England, passing over New York/Northern \nPennsylvania. As the right entrance region passes over \nPennsylvania, it will interact with a remnant MCV from \nconvection that is currently ongoing in the Midwest. This ascent\nshould allow for another round of clouds some shower \ndevelopment in the southern tier of the state later tomorrow. \n\nMain timing looks to be from the late morning to mid-afternoon. \nThe ascent may allow for some spotty afternoon showers \nelsewhere, but dry air filtering in from the north should keep \nthings fairly isolated. \n\nAs these features pass through and drier air filters in from the\nnorth, skies should start clearing Friday evening into Saturday.\nThis will make for some relatively less humid conditions to\nbegin the weekend with any isolated rain chances looking to\nremain in the southwest. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Sunday brings a return to wet conditions with \nshowers and thunderstorm chances returning. \n\nA low pressure system moving through Canada will allow a stalled\nfrontal boundary to move through Pennsylvania as a warm front \non Sunday. This will bring a moist airmass, with PWATs possibly \nexceeding 2\" into Pennsylvania. With a secondary low likely \nforming closer to Pennsylvania at the intersection of the warm \nfront and a trailing cold front, this will invigorate \ndevelopment of showers and thunderstorms. With a cold front \ndraping across Pennsylvania Sunday night, multiple rounds of \nshowers/thunderstorms may be possible into the night. \n\nAs it stands right now, ensemble guidance has the northern tier\nof Pennsylvania targeted for highest rain probabilities and\ntotals. Exact timing of this activity and how fast/slow the \ncold front moves through will likely become clearer as we \napproach Sunday. With such high PWATs and potential for multiple\nrounds/training activity, flooding potential will need to be \nwatched. Should the cold front stall/move slower, activity may \nlinger into Monday morning. After that passage, the start of \nnext week is showing signs of high pressure moving over the \nregion.",
